Use the fontconfig-backed FontConfiguration on iOS.

X11FontManager only consults FcFontConfiguration -- the one that asks
libfontconfig for the font set at runtime -- when FontUtilities.isLinux.
Our target reports as the macOS family, so it fell straight through to
MFontConfiguration, which wants a per-OS fontconfig.properties/.bfc that
JDK 21 no longer generates (make/data/fontconfig is gone). With no file to
read, FontConfiguration.head stayed null and the first text operation died
with "Fontconfig head is null, check your fonts or fonts configuration".

The native side FcFontConfiguration needs is already present: fontpath.c
builds into libawt_xawt and exports Java_sun_font_FontConfigManager_getFontConfig
and Java_sun_awt_FcFontManager_getFontPathNative, and the versioned dlopen
name expands to libfontconfig.1.dylib on this target.

X11FontManager is only reachable when XAWT is the toolkit, which on a
macOS-family build only happens in the iOS XAWT configuration, so widening
the guard to the macOS family does not change real macOS behaviour.

diff --git a/src/java.desktop/unix/classes/sun/awt/X11FontManager.java b/src/java.desktop/unix/classes/sun/awt/X11FontManager.java
index 26cf8fd6e0..d8e92927e3 100644
--- a/src/java.desktop/unix/classes/sun/awt/X11FontManager.java
+++ b/src/java.desktop/unix/classes/sun/awt/X11FontManager.java
@@ -686,7 +686,8 @@ public final class X11FontManager extends FcFontManager {
          * and do the best we can.
          */
         FontConfiguration mFontConfig = new MFontConfiguration(this);
-        if ((FontUtilities.isLinux && !mFontConfig.foundOsSpecificFile())) {
+        if (((FontUtilities.isLinux || FontUtilities.isMacOSX)
+             && !mFontConfig.foundOsSpecificFile())) {
             FcFontConfiguration fcFontConfig =
                 new FcFontConfiguration(this);
             if (fcFontConfig.init()) {
